Well, as most of you guessed, grep is an acronym.  It stands for...

                        Global Regular Expression Print,

but in case you wanted a second opinion here are some of the other
responses I
received.  Comments are from me in order to point out the different ways
one
person tries to answer the question verses another.    

Thanks goes out to everyone who responded.

-jeff braun

---------------------------------------------
General Regular Expression Parser

General Regular Expression Print

Generalized Regular Expression and Print

Get Random Expression

Get Random ExPression           (I like the capitalization here.)

Get Regular Expression

Get Regular ExPression          (Same as before)

Get Regular Expression and Print

Get Regular Expression Pattern

Global Regular Expression Print

Global Regular Expression and Print.  (The "and" makes it almost english
like.)

Global/Regular Expression/Print       (The slashs are good)

Globally find Regular Expression and Print

Global Regular Expression Parser

Grab Regular Expression and Print

Grammar Regular Expression Pattern matcher

Globally search for a Regular-Expression and Print

Group Regular Expression Print

Global search for regular expression and print out the results

Global Regular Expression Printer

Global Regular Expression Parser

Global Regular Expression Pattern

<G>lobal <R>egular <E>xpression <P>rint   (I like the "<" ">" signs)

'Globally match RE and Print (it)'.  REs are pattern matching strings like
"^[^c].*c" and "un?x$".

